I want to start this issue with saying how much I enjoy programming in S7 and the ability to directly see the type signature of an object by just looking at the code and not having to read the roxygen docs or scan the function code for `stopifnot(inherits(...))`.

I was wondering, whether S7 could be extended with a `S7_function` object, which would bring the same convenience to defining functions, i.e. properly annotated type constraints.

If one were to use `:=`, similar to how it is discussed [here](https://github.com/RConsortium/S7/issues/466), this could make function definitions look rather clean. Note that below, `ClassA` would not be the default, but the type of the input `x`.

```r
f := function(x = ClassA, y = ClassA) {
  x + y
}
```

which could be internally transformed into something like below, with additional metadata stored in the attributes as is the case for `S7`.


```r
function(x, y) {
  if (!inherits(x, ClassA)) stop("x must be of classA"),
  if (!inherits(y, ClassA)) stop("x must be of classA")
  x + y
}
```

for default arguments, one could employ the following syntax (as suggested [here](https://github.com/mb706/typer)).


```r
f := function(x = 1L : ClassA, y = 1L : ClassA) {
  x + y
}
f()
#> 2L
```

Reading the documentation of the S7 package, I saw that you can validate a property in two different ways:

1. Using the `validator` argument of the `new_class()` function:
``` r
Dog <- new_class(
  "Dog",
  properties = list(
    age = class_numeric
  ),
  validator = function(self) {
    if (self@age <= 0) {
      "@age must be a positive number."
    }
    else {
      NULL
    }
  }
)

laika <- Dog(age = -10)
#> Error: <Dog> object is invalid:
#> - @age must be a positive number.
```

2. Using the `validator` argument of the `new_property()` function:
```r
Dog <- new_class(
  "Dog",
  properties = list(
    age = new_property(
      class_numeric,
      validator = function(value) {
        if (value <= 0) {
          "Age must be a positive number."
        }
        else{
          NULL
        }
      }
    )
  )
)

beethoven <- Dog(age = 0)
#> Error: <Dog> object properties are invalid:
#> - @age Age must be a positive number.
```
It seems to me that the result is very similar between the two methods. Is there an actual difference between them ? Because I couldn't find it in the documentation. 

Also, should one way of validating be preferred over the other (in general or in specific cases) ?

Since validation steps generally tend to take a long time, I'm wondering if it's worth adding "unsafe" version of the functions for developers.

```r
# Because validation step is very expensive,
# this is a copy of `props<-`, the only difference is `check = FALSE`
`props_uncheck<-` <- function(object, value) {
  for (name in names(value)) {
    prop(object, name, check = FALSE) <- value[[name]]
  }

  object
}

# A copy of `set_props()`, using `props_uncheck<-`
# instead of `props<-` to avoid validation
set_props_uncheck <- function(object, ...) {
  props_uncheck(object) <- list(...)
  object
}
```

It may be possible to simply add the `check` argument, similar to `prop<-`, however, in that case, users would need to be aware of the risks involved, so I thought it might be better to separate the functions.

I have an issue when trying to create an object that should be a list of a specific type. I’m not sure whether the problem is in my code or in how the package works.

``` r
# Define a property that validates a list of numeric elements
prop_list_numeric <- function() {
  S7::new_property(
    class = S7::class_list,
    validator = function(value) {
      for (i in seq_along(value)) {
        val <- value[[i]]
        if (!is.numeric(val)) {
          return(paste0(
            "must be a list of <numeric>s. ",
            "Element ",
            i,
            " is not."
          ))
        }
      }
    }
  )
}

ListNumeric <- 
  S7::new_class("ListNumeric", properties = list(x = prop_list_numeric()))

# Define methods to interact with the object directly as if it were a list

S7::method(`[`, ListNumeric) <-
  function(object, ...) {
    `[`(S7::prop(object, name = "x"), ...)
  }

S7::method(`[<-`, ListNumeric) <-
  function(object, ..., value) {
    `[<-`(S7::prop(object, name = "x"), ..., value)
  }

S7::method(`[[`, ListNumeric) <-
  function(object, ...) {
    `[[`(S7::prop(object, name = "x"), ...)
  }

S7::method(`[[<-`, ListNumeric) <-
  function(object, ..., value) {
    `[[<-`(S7::prop(object, name = "x"), ..., value)
  }

# Create an instance
test_list <- ListNumeric(x = list("C" = 1:4, "D" = 5:8))

# Access and extraction behave as expected
all.equal(test_list[["D"]], test_list@x[["D"]])
#> [1] TRUE
all.equal(test_list["D"], test_list@x["D"])
#> [1] TRUE
all.equal(test_list[1], test_list@x[1])
#> [1] TRUE

# The object retains the class we defined
class(test_list)
#> [1] "ListNumeric" "S7_object"

# Validator works correctly when assigning directly to the property
test_list@x[["D"]] <- "character"
#> Error: <ListNumeric>@x must be a list of <numeric>s. Element 2 is not.
test_list@x["D"] <- "character"
#> Error: <ListNumeric>@x must be a list of <numeric>s. Element 2 is not.

# However, assigning via the custom [[<- method bypasses the validator
test_list[["D"]] <- "character"

# Moreover, the object is now a plain list, no longer an S7 object
class(test_list)
#> [1] "list"
```

